00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:06.470
So, we're going to talk a little
bit more about Android apps.

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:10.100
So, Android apps
coming on to Chrome OS.

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:12.130
First of all, let's
start with Chromebooks.

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:14.080
Chromebooks, as
you know, already

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:16.540
have this best in
class web experience.

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:20.350
They came with Chrome baked
in from the very beginning.

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:23.560
Chrome-- now the most
popular browser in the world.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:26.370
And bringing the Google
Play Store on to Chrome OS

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:29.800
has let our users do
a little bit more,

00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:33.040
from design, to
team communications,

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:36.820
to simple but critical
tasks like note taking.

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:38.860
So, now you can use
those apps as part

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:40.810
of your desktop workflow.

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:43.060
So, rather than having
to pull out a phone

00:26:43.060 --> 00:26:46.220
and refer to something on there
and have multiple devices,

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:48.700
you can just alt
tab between apps

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:51.480
which you're used to seeing
on mobile now on desktops.

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:53.410
So, integrating it part
into the same workflow

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:56.665
that you're already used to.

00:26:56.665 --> 00:26:58.490
And in fact, we've
been already working

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:03.320
with a ton of key
developers to ensure

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:06.890
that the apps that you're
starting to see on Chrome OS

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:09.639
are optimized for Chrome OS.

00:27:09.639 --> 00:27:11.930
So, some of those are internal
things, but many of them

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:13.310
are external.

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:16.070
And we're finding that as we
talk to more and more external

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:21.230
teams, that they're really
excited about extending out

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:23.780
their products, their
existing code bases,

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:26.071
into the desktop environment.

00:27:26.071 --> 00:27:28.070
Now, of course, that
environment is a little bit

00:27:28.070 --> 00:27:28.970
different to mobile.

00:27:28.970 --> 00:27:31.130
On mobile you have a
screen that's this big,

00:27:31.130 --> 00:27:33.760
and the primary mode of
interaction is touch.

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:36.680
But on the desktop,
the screen is this big,

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.570
and the primary
mode of interaction

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:41.000
is keyboard and
touchpad or mouse.

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:44.159
Now, that means that
the type of work

00:27:44.159 --> 00:27:46.700
that you're going to be doing
with that app can be different.

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:48.991
The amount of information
that comes to you on a screen

00:27:48.991 --> 00:27:51.170
that big can be a lot denser.

00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:53.600
The speed of input
with a keyboard

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:56.270
can be a lot faster
than it is with touch.

00:27:56.270 --> 00:27:59.120
And the accuracy of you're
pointing with a touchpad

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:01.400
can be much better too.

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:05.030
So, that means that
the types of activities

00:28:05.030 --> 00:28:09.760
you can do in those apps on
desktop can be different.

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:10.790
So that's one aspect.

00:28:10.790 --> 00:28:13.785
But also, the business
models can be different too.

00:28:13.785 --> 00:28:15.520
So, the really
simplistic example

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:19.330
is on mobile it's
very usual to sell

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:21.790
an app for $1, $2 at a time.

00:28:21.790 --> 00:28:24.470
And having price points
higher than that is unusual.

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:28.990
It's unusual.

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:32.770
On desktop, however, a
price point of $200 to $300,

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:33.580
for example--

00:28:33.580 --> 00:28:34.810
that's pretty normal.

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:38.620
So, that different type of
revenue model for the app,

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:42.340
and that different type of
interaction modes for the app,

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:44.890
has brought us into a
really exciting new era

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:46.400
for Android apps.

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:48.010
So, that's great for developers.

00:28:48.010 --> 00:28:50.530
And it's also great
for users, who

00:28:50.530 --> 00:28:52.900
are starting to see the Google
Play Store already coming

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:57.310
into multiple Chromebooks,
along with the millions of apps

00:28:57.310 --> 00:28:59.709
that that brings.

00:28:59.709 --> 00:29:01.250
So, rather than just
talk about this,

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:04.890
we're going to show
you the apps in action.

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:10.900
So, if we could just switch
to the Chromecast, please?

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:16.669
OK.

00:29:16.669 --> 00:29:18.460
Could we switch over
to the Chromcast view?

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:19.126
That'd be great.

00:29:26.451 --> 00:29:26.950
OK.

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:27.810
Let me see if--

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:29.250
OK, I need to recast.

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:29.980
OK.

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:32.391
Should be good.

00:29:32.391 --> 00:29:32.890
OK.

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:33.390
Perfect.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.060
Thank you.

00:29:35.060 --> 00:29:35.610
OK.

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:38.430
So, here we have an
example developer

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:40.830
that we've been working
very closely with, Slack.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:44.610
So, I'm sitting in a
room with a colleague,

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:49.880
and my colleague is
reminding me that I

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:52.430
need to dress a little
bit differently today.

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:54.506
So, I usually wear
hoodies, but today it's

00:29:54.506 --> 00:29:55.880
dress up a little
bit since we're

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:58.610
at an enterprise conference.

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:01.100
And I can reply back
and say, thanks.

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:08.145
OK.

00:30:08.145 --> 00:30:10.290
And that's an example
of an interaction

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:13.470
that we take for
granted on desktop.

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.140
I can type a message,
and hit Enter,

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.210
and it sends the message back.

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:22.200
Now, on mobile that's a
really unusual interaction

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.790
because there's
often no keyboard

00:30:23.790 --> 00:30:25.384
attached to those
mobile devices.

00:30:25.384 --> 00:30:27.300
And so, that's an example
of the kind of thing

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:30.240
that an app developer will
need to keep an eye out

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:32.940
for when we move on to desktop.

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:35.340
And my colleague, Luis, will
talk a little bit about that

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:37.524
shortly.

00:30:37.524 --> 00:30:38.940
But in this case,
I can hit enter,

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:40.606
and rather than doing
a carriage return,

00:30:40.606 --> 00:30:41.850
it'll just send the message.

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:46.970
If we could switch to
the camera, please?

00:30:46.970 --> 00:30:47.890
Thanks.

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:51.700
So, now I'm going to
show you another utility.

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:53.710
So, as well as
these kinds of apps

00:30:53.710 --> 00:30:56.050
that you're used to, like
Slack, all of the utility

00:30:56.050 --> 00:30:57.820
apps that you're
used to on mobile

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.560
are also coming to Android.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:03.220
So, as Smita mentioned
