00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:40.862
that already exist.

00:35:40.862 --> 00:35:43.070
But now, I'd like to turn
over to my colleague, Luis.

00:35:43.070 --> 00:35:44.736
He's going to tell
you a little bit more

00:35:44.736 --> 00:35:48.632
how you can optimize your apps.

00:35:48.632 --> 00:35:50.090
LUIS HÉCTOR CHÁVEZ:
Thanks, Shahid.

00:35:50.090 --> 00:35:52.012
So, my name is
Luis Héctor Chávez.

00:35:52.012 --> 00:35:53.720
I'm one of the engineers
in the team that

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:57.380
made it possible to run Android
applications in Chrome OS.

00:35:57.380 --> 00:35:59.180
So, this section of
the talk is actually

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:03.350
aimed at developers in the
room, which I think is just one.

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:05.390
And it's about the
best practices,

00:36:05.390 --> 00:36:07.220
so you can provide a
much better Android

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:09.414
experience on Chromebooks.

00:36:12.290 --> 00:36:16.430
So, first of all, we are running
a fully CTS and GTS compliant

00:36:16.430 --> 00:36:18.530
Android system on Chrome OS.

00:36:18.530 --> 00:36:20.330
This means that
most applications

00:36:20.330 --> 00:36:23.050
can run in Chrome OS without
any modifications on your part.

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:25.840
There's absolutely no
code changes required.

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:28.790
But as Smita mentioned earlier,
most Android applications

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:32.180
are optimized for touch in a
very different physical form

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:33.730
factor and device model.

00:36:33.730 --> 00:36:35.855
So, you may still want to
optimize your application

00:36:35.855 --> 00:36:38.384
so it runs even
better on Chromebooks.

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:45.760
There is a very wide variety
of Chromebook form factors.

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:49.030
Most of them will have a
physical keyboard and trackpad.

00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:51.400
Some of them will support
touching the screen.

00:36:51.400 --> 00:36:53.680
And others will support
alternative input mechanisms,

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:57.490
like the stylus
we showed earlier.

00:36:57.490 --> 00:36:59.590
While we were testing
applications, a lot of them

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:03.310
were already working great with
all of these input mechanisms.

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:06.300
Still, not all of
these applications did.

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:09.220
So, you need to
make sure that you

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:12.100
can fall back gracefully
whenever users are interacting

00:37:12.100 --> 00:37:15.790
with your app using a
keyboard, touchpad, or even

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:19.955
a physical mouse, instead
of touch or stylus.

00:37:19.955 --> 00:37:21.330
One other thing
you can consider,

00:37:21.330 --> 00:37:23.430
is that given that
most Chromebooks will

00:37:23.430 --> 00:37:26.250
have a physical keyboard,
productivity applications

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:29.190
can become even more productive
by adding some features

00:37:29.190 --> 00:37:30.390
like hot keys support.

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:37.890
In general, Chromebooks
support less amount

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:41.500
of sensors than mobile devices.

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:43.320
For instance, most
Android developers

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:47.700
are used to having a physical
GPS sensor on their devices.

00:37:47.700 --> 00:37:49.820
Chromebooks are not
equipped with one.

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:51.510
But you can still
get [? course ?]

00:37:51.510 --> 00:37:55.710
location information thanks
to the use of Wi-Fi, which

00:37:55.710 --> 00:37:59.037
works OK for most situations.

00:37:59.037 --> 00:38:01.370
Chromebooks also have partial
support for other sensors,

00:38:01.370 --> 00:38:04.270
like accelerometers
and gyroscopes.

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:05.906
For an exhaustive
list of which sensors

00:38:05.906 --> 00:38:07.280
we do support in
Chrombooks books

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:10.110
and the capabilities in
which we support them,

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:11.930
please visit
developer.android.com.

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:13.212
for the full list.

00:38:16.805 --> 00:38:18.680
Another thing that
differentiates Chromebooks

00:38:18.680 --> 00:38:21.220
from other Android
devices, is that the screen

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:25.610
is much larger and wider
in its default orientation.

00:38:25.610 --> 00:38:29.150
You will have a lot more screen
real estate available now,

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:33.106
so it's better if you make the
best use of it as possible.

00:38:33.106 --> 00:38:34.730
Make sure your
application can leverage

00:38:34.730 --> 00:38:36.830
the full width of the device,
instead of constraining

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:39.000
yourself to a narrow strip
of content in the middle,

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:42.360
like we see in
the pictures here.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:45.170
One of the top sources of
unexpected behavior of Android

00:38:45.170 --> 00:38:47.270
apps on Chromebooks
is due to the screen

00:38:47.270 --> 00:38:49.197
being a lot larger
than expected.

00:38:49.197 --> 00:38:50.780
So, please make sure
to take this into

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:52.155
account when
developing your app.

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:57.430
Using backup and
restore effectively

00:38:57.430 --> 00:38:59.440
is also very important.

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:01.920
As my colleagues have mentioned
earlier, one of the best

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:04.660
features of our Chromebooks,
is that users can just

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:07.360
grab any machine from a
pool of shared devices,

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:09.820
log into that Chromebook,
and all their applications

00:39:09.820 --> 00:39:11.780
will be there.

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:14.890
Having said that,
we, as Chrome OS,

00:39:14.890 --> 00:39:17.470
don't support synchronizing
the apps data.

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:20.350
That's something you as
app developers need to do.

00:39:20.350 --> 00:39:24.070
In order to do that, please make
sure you use Android's default

00:39:24.070 --> 00:39:26.130
backup and restore APIs.

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:31.135
That will make your users way
more happy and productive.

00:39:31.135 --> 00:39:35.340
One caveat is that local storage
on the device is not infinite.

00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:38.590
So, if too many users log
into one device, at some point

00:39:38.590 --> 00:39:41.230
we'll need to evict some of
the older accounts, together

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:42.610
with the local storage.

00:39:42.610 --> 00:39:44.470
So, please take
this into account.

00:39:49.435 --> 00:39:52.220
Chromebooks are currently
shipping with Marshmallow APIs.

00:39:52.220 --> 00:39:53.840
But you need to
make sure to plan

00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:56.780
to move to Nougat as
soon as possible-- today,

00:39:56.780 --> 00:40:00.590
if it's a possibility, since
several things will improve

00:40:00.590 --> 00:40:03.500
as soon as you move to Nougat.

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:05.970
And we will be shipping Nougat
later this year as well,

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:08.030
for Chromebooks.

00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:11.165
The most important thing is
that if you add support for it,

00:40:11.165 --> 00:40:14.150
your applications will be
able to resize arbitrarily,

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:17.436
instead of being constrained to
just one or two orientations.

00:40:17.436 --> 00:40:19.310
You'll be able to make
your applications feel

00:40:19.310 --> 00:40:23.510
more natural with the rest
of the operating system.

00:40:23.510 --> 00:40:27.540
Other APIs you'll be able to
leverage by switching to Nougat

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:30.390
are drag and drop across
applications, and mouse cursor

00:40:30.390 --> 00:40:32.240
APIs.

00:40:32.240 --> 00:40:36.047
This will improve the desktop
experience significantly.

00:40:36.047 --> 00:40:38.590
So, by being ready
for Nougat, you

00:40:38.590 --> 00:40:40.800
make sure that your
application will

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:45.450
leverage these APIs as soon
as we convert to Chromebooks.

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:46.780
And with that, back to Smita.

